{
"dur": 41.277,
"shots": [
{
"i": 1,
"start": 0.0,
"vo_start": 0.6,
"vo_end": 10.8,
"text": "So how did we get here? Rewind to 2012. Tesla launches the Model S with a seventeen-inch touchscreen in the middle of the dash, and the world loses its mind.",
"end": 11.075
},
{
"i": 2,
"start": 11.075,
"vo_start": 11.35,
"vo_end": 17.829,
"text": "The logic sounded clever. Phones got better when they lost their buttons. So surely cars would too?",
"end": 18.104
},
{
"i": 3,
"start": 18.104,
"vo_start": 18.379,
"vo_end": 28.457,
"text": "And every new model came with more features. Massage seats, ambient lighting, twelve drive modes. There simply wasn't room for a button for everything, so the screen swallowed the lot.",
"end": 28.732
},
{
"i": 4,
"start": 28.732,
"vo_start": 29.007,
"vo_end": 40.327,
"text": "But there's one big problem. A button you can feel. A screen you have to look at. And at seventy miles an hour, there's only one thing your eyes should be looking at. The road.",
"end": 41.277
}
],
